            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The climate of India is synthesized as a <strong>Tropical Monsoon Climate</strong>. While the Tropic of Cancer ($23^\\circ 30'\text{ N}$) divides the country into a northern temperate-subtropical half and a southern tropical half, the formidable orographic barrier of the Himalayas shields the north from Arctic Siberian gales, ensuring that the entire Indian landmass exhibits predominantly tropical thermal regimes.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Wladimir Köppen's Climatic Classification of India</h4>\n            <p>Köppen's empirical classification (based on monthly means of temperature, precipitation, and natural vegetation thresholds) delineates eight distinct climatic regions across India:</p>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Köppen Code</th><th>Climatic Type</th><th>Geographical Distribution in India</th><th>Salient Characteristics</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Amw</strong></td><td>Tropical Monsoon with short dry winter</td><td>Western Coastal Plain (Konkan, Malabar), Western Ghats windward scarp, Tripura.</td><td>Annual rainfall $>2,500\text{ mm}$; brief dry winter.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>As</strong></td><td>Tropical Wet with dry summer</td><td>Coromandel Coast of Tamil Nadu and adjoining coastal Andhra Pradesh.</td><td>Summer is dry; bulk of rainfall occurs during winter (October–December) from Northeast Monsoon.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Aw</strong></td><td>Tropical Savanna</td><td>Most of Peninsular India south of Tropic of Cancer (excluding Western Ghats and Coromandel).</td><td>Distinct dry winter; wet summer with rainfall $750–1,500\text{ mm}$.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>BShw</strong></td><td>Semi-Arid Steppe (Low latitude)</td><td>Rain-shadow belt of Western Ghats (interior Maharashtra, Karnataka, Rayalaseema) and parts of Gujarat, Haryana, and Rajasthan.</td><td>Rainfall $350–750\text{ mm}$; steppe vegetation, recurrent drought.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>BWhw</strong></td><td>Hot Desert (Arid)</td><td>Western Rajasthan (Thar Desert: Jaisalmer, Bikaner, Barmer) and Kutch.</td><td>Scanty rainfall ($<250\text{ mm}$); extreme temperature range; xerophytic scrub.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Cwg</strong></td><td>Humid Subtropical with dry winter and Gangetic temperature peak</td><td>Entire Indo-Gangetic Plain (Punjab, Haryana, UP, Bihar, Bengal), Malwa, and Assam plain.</td><td>Hot summers with maximum temperature before monsoon ('g' = Gangetic peak); dry cool winter.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Dfc</strong></td><td>Cold Humid Winter with short cool summer</td><td>Arunachal Pradesh, Sikkim, and parts of Assam hills.</td><td>Short mild summer ($<4\text{ months} >10^\\circ\text{C}$); heavy monsoon rainfall; cold damp winter.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>E / ET</strong></td><td>Polar / Tundra / Alpine</td><td>High Himalayas of Jammu & Kashmir, Ladakh, Himachal Pradesh, and Uttarakhand.</td><td>Warmest month $<10^\\circ\text{C}$; permafrost, alpine meadows, blizzards.</td></tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        ",
