            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The climate of the Indian subcontinent is a complex interplay of geographic, orographic, and dynamic upper-atmospheric controls.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Primary Geographic & Physical Controls</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Latitudinal Location:</strong> The <strong>Tropic of Cancer ($23^\\circ 30'\text{ N}$)</strong> passes through the exact center of India, traversing <strong>eight states</strong> (from west to east: <em>Gujarat, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Tripura, and Mizoram</em>). The southern half lies in the Tropical Zone (experiencing high solar insolation and small annual temperature ranges); the northern half lies in the Subtropical Zone.</li>\n                <li><strong>2. The Himalayan Wall:</strong> Functions as a dual climatic regulator:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Acts as an insurmountable physical shield, deflecting the freezing polar Siberian air masses away from northern India during winter.</li>\n                        <li>Acts as an orographic trap that intercepts the moisture-laden Southwest Monsoon winds, forcing them to shed their rain over the Indo-Gangetic Plains rather than escaping into Central Asia.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>3. Altitude:</strong> Places at higher elevations experience cooler temperatures (normal lapse rate of $6.5^\\circ\text{C per 1,000 m}$). For example, Agra and Darjeeling lie on roughly the same latitude ($27^\\circ\text{ N}$), but Darjeeling's mean January temperature is $4^\\circ\text{C}$ while Agra's is $16^\\circ\text{C}$.</li>\n                <li><strong>4. Distance from the Sea (Continentality):</strong> The three-sided peninsular coastline of over $7,500\text{ km}$ exerts a strong <strong>maritime moderating influence</strong> on coastal cities (Mumbai, Kochi, Chennai), resulting in equable climates with annual temperature ranges of less than $5^\\circ\text{C}$. In contrast, interior northwestern India (Delhi, Punjab, Rajasthan) experiences <strong>continentality (extreme climate)</strong>, with scorching summers ($>45^\\circ\text{C}$) and near-freezing winter nights ($<3^\\circ\text{C}$).</li>\n                <li><strong>5. Physiographic Relief & Orography:</strong> Dictates the spatial pattern of precipitation. The Western Ghats force incoming Arabian Sea monsoon winds to dump $>3,000\text{ mm}$ on the coastal strip, leaving Pune (100 km east) in a rain-shadow with only $600\text{ mm}$. Similarly, the funneling scarp of the Khasi Hills generates the world's highest rainfall at Mawsynram ($11,872\text{ mm}$).</li>\n                <li><strong>6. Upper Air Circulation & Jet Streams:</strong> The seasonal migration of the <strong>Subtropical Westerly Jet Stream</strong> to the north of the Tibetan Plateau triggers the sudden onset ('burst') of the Southwest Monsoon in early June.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
