            "title": "The Pre-Monsoon Season (Hot Weather Period): Loo, Andhi & Convection",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Hot Weather Season (Pre-Monsoon Summer)</strong> extends from <strong>March to May</strong> across the Indian subcontinent. Driven by the northward apparent migration of the sun towards the Tropic of Cancer, solar insolation increases steadily, resulting in soaring temperatures, falling barometric pressures, and hyper-thermal surface winds.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Northward March of the Maximum Thermal Belt</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>March ($~38^\\circ\text{C}$):</strong> Maximum temperature belt lies over the <strong>Deccan Plateau</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>April ($~42^\\circ\text{ to }43^\\circ\text{C}$):</strong> Thermal belt shifts north into <strong>Madhya Pradesh, Gujarat, and Vidarbha</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>May ($~45^\\circ\text{ to }48^\\circ\text{C}+$):</strong> The core of extreme heat establishes over <strong>northwestern India</strong> (Rajasthan, Punjab, Haryana, and western UP). Temperatures frequently cross $50^\\circ\text{C}$ at places like <strong>Phalodi, Churu, and Barmer</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Dreaded 'Loo' Winds</h4>\n            <p><strong>Loo</strong> refers to the fiercely hot, bone-dry, desiccating daytime winds that blow from the west and northwest across the Great Northern Plains from Punjab to Bihar during May and June:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Characteristics:</strong> Blows between 10:00 AM and sunset with velocities of $30\text{ to }40\text{ km/h}$; temperatures hover between $45^\\circ\text{ and }50^\\circ\text{C}$; relative humidity drops below $10\\%$.</li>\n                <li><strong>Physiological Impact:</strong> Direct exposure induces rapid dehydration, heat exhaustion, and fatal sunstroke (heat hyperpyrexia).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. 'Andhi' (Convective Dust Storms)</h4>\n            <p>In western Rajasthan, Haryana, and southwestern Punjab, extreme afternoon convective heating creates localized miniature low-pressure vortices. These trigger violent <strong>dust storms (Andhi)</strong>, where howling winds kick up colossal walls of blinding sand and dust, blotting out the sun and reducing visibility to zero. Because they temporarily block solar radiation and are occasionally followed by light drizzles, they bring a transient, welcome drop in temperature ($2^\\circ–4^\\circ\text{C}$).</p>\n\n            <h4>4. Coastal Equability vs Continental Furnace</h4>\n            <p>While northern and central India suffer under furnace-like continentality, coastal Peninsular India (Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Konkan) remains equable ($30^\\circ–35^\\circ\text{C}$) due to maritime breezes, although high relative humidity creates severe heat index discomfort.</p>\n        ",
