            "id": "m11",
            "title": "Pre-Monsoon Local Storms: Mango Showers, Blossom Showers & Kalbaisakhi",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>Towards the end of the hot weather season (April and May), intense diurnal surface heating destabilizes the lower atmosphere. When this heated air collides with moist maritime air currents, it unleashes severe <strong>localized convective thunderstorms</strong> known by distinct regional names across India.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Major Pre-Monsoon Thunderstorm Systems</h4>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Regional Name</th><th>Geographical Region</th><th>Meteorological Mechanism & Economic Value</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td><strong>Mango Showers<br>(Amra Varsha)</strong></td>\n                        <td>Kerala, coastal Karnataka, and parts of Tamil Nadu</td>\n                        <td>Occurs in late April and May. Crucial for preventing premature fruit drop and accelerating the <strong>early ripening of mangoes</strong>.</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td><strong>Blossom Showers<br>(Cherry Blossom)</strong></td>\n                        <td>Karnataka (Kodagu, Chikkamagaluru, Hassan) and Wayanad (Kerala)</td>\n                        <td>Brings short, sharp showers that trigger the <strong>synchronized blooming of white coffee blossoms</strong>, securing the annual coffee crop.</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td><strong>Kalbaisakhi<br>(Nor'westers)</strong></td>\n                        <td>West Bengal, Odisha, and adjoining parts of Bihar/Jharkhand</td>\n                        <td>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Meaning:</strong> <em>'Calamity of the month of Baisakh'</em> (April–May).</li>\n                                <li><strong>Genesis:</strong> Dry continental westerlies from the Chotanagpur plateau override warm, moist Bay of Bengal air, generating violent supercell squalls moving from northwest to southeast (hence <em>Nor'westers</em>).</li>\n                                <li><strong>Features:</strong> Dark anvil-topped cumulonimbus clouds, gale-force winds ($>100\text{ km/h}$), lightning, and destructive hail.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Economic Impact:</strong> While hazardous to life and property, it provides vital moisture for <strong>jute, Aus/Boro rice, and Darjeeling tea</strong>.</td>\n                            </ul>\n                        </td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td><strong>Bardoli Chheerha</strong></td>\n                        <td>Assam and the Brahmaputra Valley</td>\n                        <td>Fierce afternoon thunderstorms accompanied by squalls and heavy rain. Highly beneficial for the <strong>first flush of Assam tea leaves</strong> and summer paddy.</td>\n                    </tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Pre-monsoon thunderstorms occur towards the end of summer due to intense thermal convection interacting with moist maritime breezes.",
                "Mango Showers: Occur in Kerala and coastal Karnataka; help in the early ripening of mangoes.",
                "Blossom Showers (Cherry Blossom): Occur in Karnataka and Kerala; crucial for the blooming of coffee flowers.",
                "Kalbaisakhi (Nor'westers): Violent, destructive evening thunderstorms in West Bengal and Odisha during the month of Baisakh (April/May).",
                "Bardoli Chheerha: Spring thunderstorms in Assam; highly beneficial for early tea flush and jute sowing."
            ],
