            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The arrival of the <strong>Southwest Monsoon (June to September)</strong> is the most anticipated climatic event in South Asia, delivering over <strong>$75\\%$ of India's total annual precipitation</strong> and anchoring the Kharif agricultural cropping cycle.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The 'Burst of the Monsoon' (Monsoon Onset)</h4>\n            <p>The onset of the monsoon is not a gradual, gentle transition. It is characterized by an abrupt, violent atmospheric shift known as the <strong>'Burst of the Monsoon'</strong>:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li>After weeks of blistering pre-monsoon heat ($40^\\circ–48^\\circ\text{C}$), the arrival of oceanic westerlies causes an immediate, dramatic drop in temperature ($5^\\circ\text{ to }10^\\circ\text{C}$).</li>\n                <li>Accompanied by dark rolling skies, severe lightning, deafening thunderclaps, gale-force winds, and torrential, unceasing rains lasting several consecutive days.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. IMD Scientific Criteria for Declaring Onset over Kerala</h4>\n            <p>The <strong>India Meteorological Department (IMD)</strong> employs strict, objective tri-fold criteria to declare the official onset over Kerala:</p>\n            <ol>\n                <li><strong>Rainfall Threshold:</strong> If after 10th May, at least <strong>$60\\%$ of the 14 designated meteorological stations</strong> in Kerala, Lakshadweep, and coastal Karnataka report rainfall of <strong>$2.5\text{ mm}$ or more for two consecutive days</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Wind Field Depth:</strong> The depth of westerly/southwesterly winds must extend up to at least <strong>$600\text{ hPa}$</strong> in the equatorial Arabian Sea ($0^\\circ–10^\\circ\text{ N}, 55^\\circ–75^\\circ\text{ E}$).</li>\n                <li><strong>Outgoing Longwave Radiation (OLR):</strong> Satellite-derived OLR must drop below <strong>$200\text{ W/m}^2$</strong>, confirming dense, deep convective cumulonimbus cloud cover.</li>\n            </ol>\n\n            <h4>3. Standard Normal Dates of Monsoon Arrival across India</h4>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Region / Station</th><th>Normal Date of Arrival</th><th>Branch Responsible</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Andaman & Nicobar Islands</strong></td><td><strong>May 15 – 20</strong></td><td>Bay of Bengal Branch (Earliest in India)</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Kerala (Mainland India Onset)</strong></td><td><strong>June 1</strong></td><td>Arabian Sea Branch</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Northeast India (Assam, Meghalaya)</strong></td><td><strong>June 5</strong></td><td>Bay of Bengal Branch</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Mumbai & Goa</strong></td><td><strong>June 10 – 11</strong></td><td>Arabian Sea Branch</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Kolkata & Patna</strong></td><td><strong>June 10 – 15</strong></td><td>Bay of Bengal Branch</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Central India (Nagpur, Bhopal)</strong></td><td><strong>June 15 – 20</strong></td><td>Combined Branches</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Delhi & Western UP</strong></td><td><strong>June 27 – 29</strong></td><td>Combined (Arabian Sea & Bay branches meet)</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Entire Country Covered</strong></td><td><strong>July 8</strong></td><td>Final coverage over western Rajasthan</td></tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        ",
