            "title": "Southwest Monsoon: The Bay of Bengal Branch & The Gangetic Deflection",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Bay of Bengal Branch</strong> of the Southwest Monsoon collects copious moisture as it traverses the warm waters of the Bay of Bengal. Blocked from proceeding eastward by the mountain barrier of Myanmar, it splits into two dynamic arms upon striking the head of the Bay:</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Sub-Branch 1: The Northeast Funnel & World Record Deluge</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Moves directly north-northeast into the <strong>Meghalaya Plateau and the Brahmaputra Valley</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Funneling Amphitheater:</strong> The southern scarp of the <strong>Khasi Hills</strong> features steep, narrow V-shaped gorges opening south towards the flooded Sylhet plains of Bangladesh. Trapped inside this mountain amphitheater, the moisture-saturated air is forced into abrupt vertical ascent, cooling adiabatically.</li>\n                <li><strong>Mawsynram & Cherrapunji:</strong> <strong>Mawsynram (11,872 mm)</strong> and <strong>Cherrapunji / Sohra (11,430 mm)</strong> record the highest average annual precipitation on Earth.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Sub-Branch 2: The Gangetic Deflection & The Westward Gradient</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Arakan Yoma Deflection:</strong> The high mountain wall of the <strong>Arakan Yoma (Myanmar)</strong> blocks the eastern expansion of the monsoon, forcing the entire maritime current to deflect sharply to the <strong>left (westward and northwestward)</strong> into the Indo-Gangetic Plains.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Himalayan Guiding Wall:</strong> Hemmed in between the Himalayas on the north and the Peninsular Highlands on the south, this easterly current flows up the Gangetic trough towards the low-pressure cell in northwestern India.</li>\n                <li><strong>The East-to-West Rainfall Gradient:</strong> Because the winds blow away from their maritime source in the Bay of Bengal, their moisture content diminishes steadily as they travel westward along the plains:\n                    <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                        <thead>\n                            <tr><th>Station (East to West)</th><th>Approx. Annual Monsoon Rainfall</th><th>Distance from Bay of Bengal</th></tr>\n                        </thead>\n                        <tbody>\n                            <tr><td><strong>Kolkata</strong></td><td>$1,600\text{ mm}$ ($160\text{ cm}$)</td><td>Coastal head of Bay</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td><strong>Patna</strong></td><td>$1,050\text{ mm}$ ($105\text{ cm}$)</td><td>$\\sim 500\text{ km}$ inland</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td><strong>Prayagraj (Allahabad)</strong></td><td>$900\text{ mm}$ ($90\text{ cm}$)</td><td>$\\sim 750\text{ km}$ inland</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td><strong>Kanpur</strong></td><td>$800\text{ mm}$ ($80\text{ cm}$)</td><td>$\\sim 900\text{ km}$ inland</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td><strong>Delhi</strong></td><td>$650\text{ mm}$ ($65\text{ cm}$)</td><td>$\\sim 1,300\text{ km}$ inland</td></tr>\n                        </tbody>\n                    </table>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The Union of the Two Branches in Punjab-Haryana</h4>\n            <p>Around late June, the western extremity of the <strong>Bay of Bengal Branch</strong> unites with the Arabian Sea Branch over the plains of <strong>Haryana, Punjab, and western Uttar Pradesh</strong>, jointly delivering the monsoon rains to Delhi and the western Himalayan foothills.</p>\n        ",
