            "title": "The Northeast Monsoon (Winter Monsoon) & The Coromandel Coast",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>While the rest of India experiences dry, sunny weather during winter, the southeastern littoral—specifically the <strong>Coromandel Coast of Tamil Nadu, Puducherry, and southern coastal Andhra Pradesh</strong>—experiences its primary rainy season under the <strong>Northeast Monsoon (October to December)</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Meteorological Mechanism of the Northeast Monsoon</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Planetary Reversal:</strong> As the ITCZ migrates south of the Equator into the southern Indian Ocean, the high-pressure system over Central Asia and northwestern India re-establishes the normal <strong>Northeast Trade Winds</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Moisture Pick-up over Bay of Bengal:</strong> Over northern and central India, these winds blow over dry land and are completely dry. However, the stream blowing across the <strong>Bay of Bengal</strong> absorbs vast quantities of sensible heat and moisture from the warm sea surface ($28^\\circ–29^\\circ\text{C}$).</li>\n                <li><strong>Orographic & Coastal Landfall:</strong> Upon reaching the Tamil Nadu coast, these northeast winds encounter the low escarpment of the Eastern Ghats and the thermal convergence of the land-sea boundary. They release copious, prolonged rainfall (often exceeding <strong>$800\text{ to }1,200\text{ mm}$</strong> across Chennai, Cuddalore, Nagapattinam, and Thanjavur).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Agricultural & Socio-Economic Lifeline of Tamil Nadu</h4>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Southwest Monsoon (June–Sept)</th><th>Northeast Monsoon (Oct–Dec)</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Share of Tamil Nadu's Rain</strong></td><td>Only $\\sim 32–35\\%$ (Rain-shadow deficit).</td><td><strong>$\\sim 48–50\\%$ (The primary agrarian lifeline).</strong></td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Key Crops Sustained</strong></td><td>Dryland millets, cotton, pulses.</td><td><strong>Samba paddy crop in the Kaveri Delta</strong>; tank replenishment.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Associated Hazards</strong></td><td>Minor local thunderstorms.</td><td>Severe tropical cyclones, urban flooding in Chennai (e.g., Dec 2015 floods, Cyclone Michaung 2023).</td></tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>3. Dry Continental Phase over Northern India</h4>\n            <p>Throughout northern, western, and central India, the Northeast Monsoon winds blow exclusively from land to sea. With zero moisture source, these continental winds produce cloudless blue skies, crisp dry air, low night temperatures, and sunny winter days.</p>\n        ",
