            "id": "m19",
            "title": "Spatial Distribution of Rainfall across India",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>India exhibits profound <strong>spatial heterogeneity in rainfall distribution</strong>. While the national average annual rainfall hovers around <strong>$118\text{ to }120\text{ cm}$</strong>, local precipitation ranges from over $11,000\text{ mm}$ in Meghalaya to less than $100\text{ mm}$ in the cold desert of Ladakh and the sandy dunes of Jaisalmer.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Four Rainfall Zones of India</h4>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Rainfall Zone</th><th>Annual Precipitation</th><th>Geographical Coverage</th><th>Dominant Vegetation & Agronomy</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td><strong>1. Areas of Heavy Rainfall</strong></td>\n                        <td><strong>$>200\text{ cm}$</strong> ($>2,000\text{ mm}$)</td>\n                        <td>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li>Western Coastal Plain and windward slopes of <strong>Western Ghats</strong> ($250–600\text{ cm}$).</li>\n                                <li><strong>Meghalaya Plateau</strong> ($>1,000\text{ cm}$ at Mawsynram and Cherrapunji).</li>\n                                <li>Arunachal Pradesh, Assam foothills, northern West Bengal (Duars).</li>\n                                <li><strong>Andaman & Nicobar Islands</strong> and Lakshadweep.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </td>\n                        <td>Tropical Wet Evergreen & Semi-Evergreen Rainforests; intensive paddy, tea, rubber, and spices.</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td><strong>2. Areas of Moderate Rainfall</strong></td>\n                        <td><strong>$100\text{ to }200\text{ cm}$</strong></td>\n                        <td>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li>Eastern Peninsular Plateau: Odisha, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, eastern MP.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Middle and Lower Ganga Plains</strong> (Bihar, West Bengal).</li>\n                                <li>Brahmaputra Valley (Assam) and parts of eastern Ghats.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </td>\n                        <td>Moist Deciduous Forests (Sal, Teak); primary rice-producing belt; prone to recurrent flooding.</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td><strong>3. Areas of Low Rainfall</strong></td>\n                        <td><strong>$50\text{ to }100\text{ cm}$</strong></td>\n                        <td>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Upper Ganga Plain</strong> (western UP, Haryana, Punjab).</li>\n                                <li>Eastern Rajasthan (Mewar, Aravalli fringes).</li>\n                                <li><strong>Deccan Rain-Shadow Plateau:</strong> Interior Maharashtra, northern Karnataka, Telangana, and Rayalaseema.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </td>\n                        <td>Dry Deciduous Forests and Open Thorn Scrub; wheat, millets (jowar, bajra), cotton, pulses, and oilseeds.</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td><strong>4. Areas of Scanty Rainfall</strong></td>\n                        <td><strong>$<50\text{ cm}$</strong> ($<500\text{ mm}$)</td>\n                        <td>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Thar Desert of Western Rajasthan:</strong> Jaisalmer ($<15\text{ cm}$), Bikaner, Barmer.</li>\n                                <li>The Rann of Kutch (Gujarat).</li>\n                                <li><strong>Cold Desert of Ladakh:</strong> Leh ($<10\text{ cm}$), Spiti Valley (Himachal).</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </td>\n                        <td>Xerophytic thorny bushes, acacia, date palms; pastoralism; heavily dependent on canal irrigation (Indira Gandhi Canal).</td>\n                    </tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>2. Variability of Rainfall (Coefficient of Variation)</h4>\n            <p>Rainfall variability is <strong>inversely proportional to the total amount of rainfall</strong>: areas of heavy rainfall (Western Ghats, Northeast) have low variability ($<15–20\\%$), ensuring reliable annual rains. Conversely, areas of low rainfall (Rajasthan, Kutch, Rayalaseema) have extreme variability ($>40–50\\%$), making them chronic hotspots for flash droughts and agricultural devastation.</p>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "India's mean annual rainfall is ~120 cm (~1,180 mm), displaying extreme spatial heterogeneity.",
                "Areas of High Rainfall (>200 cm): Western Ghats windward scarp (250–600 cm), Meghalaya Plateau (>1,000 cm), Arunachal Pradesh, Sub-Himalayan West Bengal, and Andaman & Nicobar.",
                "Areas of Medium Rainfall (100–200 cm): Eastern part of Peninsular plateau (Odisha, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh), Middle & Lower Ganga Plains, Assam Valley.",
                "Areas of Low Rainfall (50–100 cm): Upper Ganga Plain, Haryana, Punjab, eastern Rajasthan, Deccan rain-shadow interior (Maharashtra, Karnataka, Rayalaseema).",
                "Areas of Inadequate / Scanty Rainfall (<50 cm): Western Rajasthan (Thar Desert), Kutch, and Cold Desert of Ladakh."
            ],
