            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>Due to its unique tropical geography, vast peninsular coastline, and high population density, India is extraordinarily vulnerable to <strong>extreme weather events and hydrometeorological disasters</strong>, a fragility now being acutely magnified by anthropogenic climate change.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Spectrum of Indian Droughts</h4>\n            <p>Approximately <strong>$68\\%$ of India's net sown agricultural area</strong> is vulnerable to drought in varying degrees. The <strong>National Commission on Agriculture (1976)</strong> categorizes droughts into three progressive stages:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Meteorological Drought:</strong> Occurs when seasonal precipitation is deficient by <strong>$>25\\%$ of the long-term normal</strong> (severe if $>50\\%$).</li>\n                <li><strong>2. Hydrological Drought:</strong> Prolonged meteorological drought leads to the depletion of surface water bodies (drying up of rivers, reservoirs, tanks) and sharp lowering of the water table.</li>\n                <li><strong>3. Agricultural Drought:</strong> Inadequate soil moisture during critical plant growth stages triggers permanent wilting, catastrophic crop failure, and fodder scarcity.</li>\n                <li><strong>Chronic Drought Zones:</strong> Bundelkhand (UP/MP), Marathwada and Vidarbha (Maharashtra), Rayalaseema (AP), and North Interior Karnataka.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Flood Hazards in India</h4>\n            <p>Over <strong>$40\text{ million hectares}$ ($12\\%$ of India's total land area)</strong> is prone to recurrent annual inundations:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Brahmaputra Basin (Assam):</strong> Exacerbated by riverbed aggradation from the 1950 earthquake, severe braiding, and excessive monsoon rainfall in the surrounding mountains.</li>\n                <li><strong>Ganga Basin (Bihar and UP):</strong> Driven by Himalayan tributaries (Kosi, Gandak, Ghaghara) carrying colossal silt loads, resulting in river avulsion, back-swamp flooding (Tals), and embankment breaches.</li>\n                <li><strong>Urban Flooding:</strong> Driven by unregulated encroachment of natural lake beds, concrete impermeable surfaces, and inadequate stormwater drains (e.g., Mumbai 2005, Chennai 2015/2023, Bengaluru 2022).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Emerging Climate Change Signatures over India</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Shift in Rainfall Characteristics:</strong> Studies by the Ministry of Earth Sciences (MoES) confirm that while total seasonal monsoon rainfall remains relatively stable, its distribution has become highly erratic: <strong>short-duration, high-intensity cloudburst events ($>100–150\text{ mm/day}$) have tripled</strong>, while the number of rainy days has shrunk, accompanied by prolonged dry breaks.</li>\n                <li><strong>Arabian Sea Rapid Intensification:</strong> The historically tranquil Arabian Sea has experienced rapid warming ($>1.2^\\circ–1.4^\\circ\text{C}$), generating frequent, severe tropical cyclones (Cyclone Ockhi, Nisarga, Tauktae, Biparjoy) that undergo sudden rapid intensification before landfall.</li>\n                <li><strong>Himalayan Cryosphere Retreat:</strong> Accelerated glacier melting expands proglacial moraine lakes, heightening the risk of catastrophic <strong>Glacial Lake Outburst Floods (GLOFs)</strong> downstream.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
